  <section id="metrics">
    <h2>1. Task and metrics</h2>
    <p>
      The loader produces one gold concept identifier per retained row. This benchmark ranks concepts
      that have training text, supplied by annotated training mentions or dictionary names.
    </p>
    <p>
      Total means rows loaded by the PubTator reader before filtering to the training vocabulary.
      Annotations already skipped by the reader are outside this denominator. Metrics follow XMR4EL
      evaluation protocol <strong>1</strong>.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Acc@1</dt>
      <dd>
        The fraction of in-vocabulary rows whose first prediction equals the gold identifier.
        Gold concepts without training text are excluded. Kept / loaded counts appear in the
        dataset-size table.
      </dd>
      <dt>Acc@1_all <span>“cov × acc” in the report</span></dt>
      <dd>Correct first predictions / all loaded rows, counting out-of-vocabulary gold concepts as misses.</dd>
      <dt>Vocabulary coverage</dt>
      <dd>The proportion of loaded rows that are in vocabulary (kept / loaded).</dd>
      <dt>MRR</dt>
      <dd>Mean reciprocal rank of the gold concept over in-vocabulary rows; missing gold contributes zero.</dd>
      <dt>Recall@10 <span>R@10</span></dt>
      <dd>The fraction of in-vocabulary rows with the gold concept in the first ten predictions.</dd>
      <dt>Candidate recall <span>R@cand</span></dt>
      <dd>The fraction of in-vocabulary rows with the gold concept anywhere in the returned candidate scores.</dd>
      <dt>Mention-string groups</dt>
      <dd>
        Acc@1 per in-vocabulary group. Keys use text before <code>[SEP]</code>, stripped and lowercased
        after the configured abbreviation expansion.
        <ul>
          <li><b>Seen 1</b>: the key occurs in training with one distinct label.</li>
          <li><b>Seen &gt;1</b>: it occurs with several labels.</li>
          <li><b>Unseen</b>: it does not occur in training.</li>
        </ul>
      </dd>
      <dt>Hybrid <span>diagnostic</span></dt>
      <dd>
        A seen mention key maps to its majority training label; otherwise the system's first prediction
        is used. The denominator is in-vocabulary rows.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      These scores are not comparable with published numbers that score all mentions against a full
      ontology.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="protocol">
    <h2>2. Systems and evaluation protocol</h2>
    <h3>XMR4EL</h3>
    <p>
      Both recorded trees use <code>configs/xmr4el_bc5cdr_config.json</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Representations:</strong> SapBERT (<code>cambridgeltl/SapBERT-from-PubMedBERT-fulltext</code>,
        CLS) + character 2–4-gram TF-IDF (SVD 768) + word-unigram context TF-IDF (10-token window,
        SVD 768), 2,304 dimensions. Blocks are normalized before concatenation. Abbreviation expansion
        uses <code>append</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hierarchy:</strong> PIFA label embeddings, balanced k-means with 128 clusters, depth 2,
        joint logistic-regression matchers with balanced class weights.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Search:</strong> beam 10, 100 candidates per visited leaf, no final top-k cap. Leaf
        matcher probabilities are multiplied by ancestor routing-path probabilities, then reweighted
        using mention similarity with beta 10. The recorded runs return 1,000 candidates per query.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Baselines and references</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>SapBERT 1-NN:</strong> each label is scored by maximum cosine similarity to its training
        mention embeddings (<code>scripts/baselines/nn_baselines.py</code>). It uses the saved tree's
        training rows, vocabulary and transformer configuration; exact ties go to the lower label index.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Character TF-IDF 1-NN:</strong> the same label scoring using unreduced character TF-IDF
        fitted on training mentions. This string-similarity reference does not use the tree's SVD or
        context blocks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>PECOS XR-Linear:</strong> libpecos 1.2.8 trained on XMR4EL's exported features and aligned
        labels (<code>scripts/baselines/pecos_compare.py</code>), <code>-nr_splits 16 -max_leaf_size 100
        -threshold 0</code>, beam 10, top 100. PECOS is trained once on the training rows (the tree’s train
        split + dictionary rows) packaged in the dev export; dev and test mentions are only predicted, the
        test set with that same model. It has no nearest-neighbour reweighting. This compares complete
        configured systems on shared features — XR-Linear against XMR4EL’s tree, leaf matcher and
        reweighting. Its hierarchy, loss, scoring and candidate budget differ from XMR4EL's.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Training resources</h3>
    <p>
      Both training files are <code>train_plus_ctd.pubtator</code>: the train split plus CTD MEDIC/OMIM
      for NCBI-disease, and the train split plus CTD chemicals for BC5CDR-chemical. Dictionary assembly
      and corpus contents were not independently audited.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ties follow each system's implemented ordering; identical tie handling across systems is not
      guaranteed. Shared features do not isolate the ranking algorithm or reproduce published PECOS
      pipelines.
    </p>
  </section>

