  <section id="limits">
    <h2>6. Evidence and limitations</h2>
    <p>
      The recorded metrics and logs were checked in the local validation bundle, including clean-code
      records, configuration bytes, recorded input hashes, label counts and loaded row counts. The bundle
      contains run metadata, metric JSONs, baseline reports and PECOS score logs; these numerical artifacts
      are not included in a source checkout.
    </p>
    <p>
      Input hashes were cross-checked as recorded metadata. Corpus contents, dictionary assembly, split
      independence and possible dictionary leakage were not independently audited. The bundle omits the
      saved trees, corpora, fitted transformer revision metadata, exported feature matrices, PECOS models
      and prediction matrices, so individual predictions and export alignment cannot be recomputed from it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Baseline reports and PECOS logs match vocabulary, row and mention-group counts and follow the
      source-defined filtering/export contract. They lack the protocol, population and mention-group hashes
      required by the terminal interface's strict comparison checks. Matching counts alone do not prove
      identical evaluated rows; the tables describe the recorded outputs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both datasets used the same configuration. The project reports that neither was used for tuning;
      the saved records verify the common configuration, not that tuning history. The test files had
      been evaluated before this validation.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is one trained tree per dataset, with one regular test evaluation and the fixed beta-zero
      ablation. No repeated-seed estimate or statistical-significance test is reported. The ambiguous
      mention-key groups contain only 17–111 in-vocabulary rows per split.
    </p>
    <p>
      Shared features do not make the systems' hierarchies, losses, score fusion or candidate budgets
      equivalent. The tables compare configured systems. Candidate recall can lose gold labels through
      routing or the per-leaf candidate limit; the reported scores do not isolate either cause.
      The reweighting ablation measures the effect of that step; establishing the hierarchy's independent
      contribution requires a separate controlled comparison.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reproduce">
    <h2>7. Reproduction</h2>
    <p>From the XMR4EL repository root, with the prerequisites below:</p>
    <pre><code>mkdir -p outputs
nohup bash scripts/run_validation.sh &gt; outputs/validation_run.log 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;</code></pre>
    <h3>Prerequisites</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Linux, Docker with CUDA GPU access, and access to the SapBERT checkpoint or an existing cache.</li>
      <li>
        Local datasets <code>datasets/ncbi_disease</code> and <code>datasets/BC5CDR/chemical</code>, each
        containing <code>train_plus_ctd.pubtator</code>, <code>dev.pubtator</code> and <code>test.pubtator</code>
        matching the script's pinned hashes.
      </li>
      <li>A clean tracked checkout and the model configuration matching the script's pinned hash.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The script builds the <code>xmr4el</code> and <code>xmr4el-pecos</code> Docker images and refuses
      existing <code>outputs/validation/</code> or <code>outputs/pecos/validation/</code> directories.
      Environment variables at its top control CPU allocation, thread count, RAM, scheduling and GPUs.
    </p>
    <h3>Outputs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>outputs/validation/</code>: logs, environment, baseline reports and run specifications.</li>
      <li><code>outputs/runs/&lt;timestamp&gt;_val_&lt;set&gt;[_test|_knn0]</code>: tracked run records and metrics.</li>
      <li>Saved XMR4EL trees and PECOS exports, models and predictions.</li>
      <li><code>outputs/validation.tar.gz</code>: run records and logs, excluding trees and PECOS exports/models/predictions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The recorded source commit is <code>aeca7a4</code>. Some Docker dependencies and the embedding-cache
      state are not pinned, so a new build and run may produce different timings or predictions.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
